    "subtitle": "How to prepare for your US student visa interview, what officers actually ask, and the documents that matter most.",
    "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1523050854058-8df90110c9f1?auto=format&fit=crop&w=400w=1200&q=70q=70",
    "author": "Ayesha Rai",
    "date": "3 September 2026",
    "content": "<p>How to prepare for your US student visa interview, what officers actually ask, and the documents that matter most. This guide is written for students who want to walk into the embassy confident and well-prepared.</p><h2>Before the interview</h2><p>Organise your documents in the order you are likely to need them: passport, I-20, admission letter, financial proof, and ties to your home country. Practice explaining your study plan in one minute without reading from a script.</p><h2>During the interview</h2><p>Answer honestly and keep your responses short. The officer is not testing your English perfection; they want to know you are a genuine student with a clear plan. Avoid memorised speeches.</p><h2>After the interview</h2><p>Whether approved or asked for administrative processing, know the next steps. Keep copies of everything, and do not book non-refundable travel until your passport is back with the visa.</p>"
